/*
 * HI_RES_AWARE -- opt out of Windows Mobile's DPI virtualisation.
 *
 * Windows Mobile 2003 SE through 6.5 assume an executable without this resource
 * was written for QVGA (240x320) and give it a virtual coordinate space at half
 * scale on a VGA or WVGA panel, then scale its GDI output up by two on the way
 * to the framebuffer.  On the HD2's 480x800 that means:
 *
 *   GetSystemMetrics(SM_CXSCREEN)  ->  240  (not 480)
 *   GetSystemMetrics(SM_CYSCREEN)  ->  400  (not 800)
 *   GetDeviceCaps(hdc, LOGPIXELSX) ->  96   (the lie; the panel is 192)
 *
 * GDI honours that scale.  EGL and GL do not: the window is created in virtual
 * pixels, eglCreateWindowSurface hands back a surface of that many *physical*
 * pixels, and the driver blits it 1:1 into the top-left corner of the panel.
 * Meanwhile the touch panel digitises in physical pixels and WM_LBUTTONDOWN
 * arrives already divided by two.  The game therefore drew into a quarter of the
 * screen while taps landed at twice the distance from the origin that they
 * looked -- the upper-left of the rendered image is reachable, the rest of the
 * image is not, and the lower-right three quarters of the panel are live but
 * blank.  That is issue 2 in the first on-device report, and this file is the
 * fix; the scaling fallback in main_winmobile.h only exists in case a device
 * ignores the resource.
 *
 * Format: resource ID 1, type 24, body a single 2-byte WORD with the value 1.
 * Type 24 is RT_MANIFEST on desktop Windows; Windows CE's loader reads the same
 * slot under the name CEUX and treats a non-zero WORD as "this application
 * handles its own scaling".  The numeric 24 is spelled out rather than written
 * as CEUX because binutils' windres has no such keyword -- it only knows the
 * desktop RT_* names -- and this is the form eVC's rc.exe emits anyway.
 *
 * The consequence of claiming it is that nothing scales the UI for us any more.
 * That is what we want here and it costs nothing: the game lays out every screen
 * from Minecraft::width/height at runtime (Gui::setSize, Screen::init) and
 * hardcodes no pixel geometry, so it simply gets 800x480 to work with instead of
 * 400x240.  Font and GUI scale come from Options/Gui, not from the OS DPI.
 *
 * There is no accompanying STRINGTABLE, ICON or VERSIONINFO on purpose: this is
 * the whole resource script, and every byte it adds to the image is these two.
 *
 * LANGUAGE 0, 0 -- LANG_NEUTRAL/SUBLANG_NEUTRAL -- rather than windres's default
 * of 0x0409 (English, US).  The loader looks this resource up by name and type
 * and does not care what language it is filed under *if* its FindResource falls
 * back the way the desktop one does; if it instead insists on the UI language or
 * neutral, a resource filed under en-US is invisible on a localised ROM, and the
 * device this port is tested on runs a Russian build of WM 6.5.  Neutral is
 * found in either case, so there is no reason to file it anywhere else.
 *
 * All of which is theory: the HD2 in front of this port ignores the resource
 * however it is filed, and still reports 400x240 at 96 dpi.  The port therefore
 * measures the panel at startup and sizes its window from that -- see the
 * display-scaling block in main_winmobile.h.  This file stays because it is the
 * documented mechanism, it costs two bytes, and on a device that honours it the
 * measurement finds a factor of 1 and every part of that fallback becomes the
 * identity.
 */

LANGUAGE 0, 0

1 24 DISCARDABLE
BEGIN
	0x0001
END
